meta {
  name: Create Quote Ticket Lead
  type: http
  seq: 17
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

script:pre-request {
  // A fresh identity every run. The intake pipeline dedupes on the contact and
  // the address, so a fixed one would resolve to the *same* lead on the second
  // run — and that lead already carries the quote ticket the next request is
  // supposed to open.
  bru.setVar("quoteTicketStamp", String(Date.now()));
}

body:json {
  {
    "primaryContact": {
      "firstName": "Quinn",
      "lastName": "Ticket{{quoteTicketStamp}}",
      "dateOfBirth": "1986-02-14",
      "phone": "(555) 707-3131",
      "email": "quinn.ticket{{quoteTicketStamp}}@example.com"
    },
    "address": {
      "street": "{{quoteTicketStamp}} Quote Ticket Way",
      "city": "Tulsa",
      "state": "OK",
      "zip": "74104"
    },
    "members": [],
    "policiesOfInterest": [{ "policyType": "Auto", "itemCount": 1 }],
    "leadSourceCode": "WCO7l",
    "submissionToken": "{{$guid}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setVar("quoteTicketLeadId", res.getBody().id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("is a genuinely new lead, not a dedupe match", function () {
    // If this ever came back `alreadyExisted`, the next request would be
    // asserting against a ticket some earlier run opened.
    expect(res.getBody().alreadyExisted).to.not.equal(true);
  });
}

docs {
  # Create Quote Ticket Lead — a lead with no quote ticket yet

  Not an endpoint of its own; a fixture, and the same precedent as **Create
  Disposable Lead** and **Share Links › Create Disposable Share Link**.

  **Open Lead Service Ticket** asserts that the ticket it gets back is `open`,
  unresolved, and assigned to the caller. All three are only true for a lead
  whose ticket is being created *now*, and the endpoint is deliberately
  idempotent — one quote ticket per lead, ever, so a second call returns the
  existing one whatever state it has reached.

  Pointing it at `{{leadId}}` (the newest lead in the list) was what made it
  fail: on any re-run that is a lead an earlier run had already carried through
  **Sold Deals**, whose sale resolved the quote ticket. The request then read
  back `status: "resolved"` and correctly failed its own assertion.

  The timestamp in the contact and the street is load-bearing: `LeadIntakeService`
  dedupes on the primary contact and the address, so a fixed identity would
  resolve to the same lead every run and reintroduce exactly the same problem.
}
